Yes—this works better if the joke is not “acting gay is funny,” but that every contestant is desperately performing what they think the show expects, while the actual hidden mechanic is different from what any of them believe. Cage, naturally, treats it as espionage.

Nicolas Cage woke up on a white leather couch beneath a neon sign that read:

WHO’S THE STRAIGHT GUY?

He stared at it for several seconds.

Then at the cameras.

Then at the champagne.

Then at the seven men seated around him.

One wore a silk shirt open to the sternum.

One had perfectly sculpted eyebrows.

One was built like a refrigerator and was holding a tiny Pomeranian.

One looked like a retired accountant.

One was British.

One was Nicolas Cage.

Not him.

Another Nicolas Cage.

Specifically, the version of Nicolas Cage from approximately 1997.

Longer hair.

Leather jacket.

Very intense.

Present-day Cage slowly pointed at him.

“No.”

1997 Cage pointed back.

“No.”

The host stepped into the room.

He was handsome in a highly engineered way.

“Gentlemen! Welcome to Who’s the Straight Guy?”

Confetti exploded.

The Pomeranian barked.

Present-day Cage flinched.

The host continued.

“Seven contestants.”

Cage counted.

Eight.

He looked at the other Cage.

The other Cage counted too.

Also got eight.

Both said at the same time:

“There are eight of us.”

The host ignored them.

“One secret straight man.”

A dramatic sting played.

“Seven gay men.”

Another sting.

“And one contestant pretending.”

Cage looked around.

Everyone else began looking around too.

The accountant crossed his legs.

The bodybuilder stroked the Pomeranian.

The British man quietly said, “Oh dear.”

The host grinned.

“Your job is simple: identify the straight man before he fools you all.”

Cage raised a hand.

“Yes, Nicolas?”

“Which one?”

The host blinked.

“What?”

“There are two Nicolas Cages.”

The other Cage raised his hand.

“Three.”

Everyone turned.

A man at the minibar removed his sunglasses.

He was another Nicolas Cage.

This one looked approximately forty years old and had a mustache.

Present Cage closed his eyes.

“Of course.”

The host looked at his cue card.

“That… was not in rehearsal.”

Mustache Cage sipped a martini.

“Nothing worth doing is.”

The host recovered.

“Contestants, your first challenge begins now!”

—

The first challenge was called:

BRUNCH INTUITION

Each man had to order brunch.

No menu.

They were simply told to “order authentically.”

This caused immediate psychological collapse.

The accountant stared at the waiter.

“What does that mean?”

The waiter said nothing.

The bodybuilder whispered:

“Just be yourself.”

The accountant snapped:

“I DON’T KNOW WHAT THAT MEANS ANYMORE.”

The British man ordered eggs Benedict.

Everyone stared at him.

He became alarmed.

“What?”

Silk Shirt leaned back.

“Interesting.”

“What’s interesting?”

“Nothing.”

“No, you said interesting.”

“I said interesting.”

“Why?”

“I can’t tell you.”

“You don’t know, do you?”

Silk Shirt quietly drank his mimosa.

Present-day Cage watched all of this.

He understood immediately.

Nobody here was relaxed.

Everyone was performing.

Which meant—

He leaned toward 1997 Cage.

“They’re all straight.”

1997 Cage whispered:

“That’s impossible.”

“Why?”

“The host said seven gay men.”

“Hosts lie.”

1997 Cage absorbed this.

“Television lies.”

“Yes.”

“Reality television lies twice.”

Cage nodded.

“Now you’re getting it.”

Mustache Cage appeared between them.

“You’re both wrong.”

They turned.

Mustache Cage whispered:

“There is no straight man.”

Present Cage narrowed his eyes.

“There’s always a straight man.”

“That’s what they want you to think.”

1997 Cage said:

“Who is ‘they’?”

Mustache Cage sipped his drink.

“The producers.”

Present Cage leaned closer.

“What do the producers want?”

Mustache Cage looked around.

“Content.”

All three Cages went silent.

This was plausible.

—

The second challenge was called:

THE CLOSET

The contestants entered a walk-in closet filled with clothing.

Each man had to choose an outfit for another contestant.

The host explained that “fashion instincts can reveal hidden truths.”

The bodybuilder immediately panicked.

“I wear gym shorts.”

Silk Shirt said:

“That tracks.”

“WHAT DOES THAT MEAN?”

“Nobody knows what anything means anymore,” said the accountant.

The British man was assigned to dress Present Cage.

He selected:

black slacks,

a black turtleneck,

a camel overcoat,

and loafers.

Cage looked at the outfit.

“What is this?”

The Brit said:

“Elegant.”

“It’s suspicious.”

“It’s a coat.”

“It’s a narrative.”

“It is literally a coat.”

Cage held it up.

“Why camel?”

“Because black would be too severe.”

Cage stared at him.

The British man stared back.

“You’re very intense.”

“So is mustard.”

“That sentence has no meaning.”

“Exactly.”

—

Behind the mirrored glass, the producers were delighted.

One assistant producer whispered:

“This is incredible.”

Another said:

“They all think there’s one straight guy.”

“There isn’t.”

“I know.”

“Do they know they’re all straight?”

“No.”

“And they all think everyone else is gay?”

“Yes.”

“And now the Cages think they’ve uncovered the twist?”

“Yes.”

“What’s the actual twist?”

The executive producer smiled.

“There’s one contestant who knows the real premise.”

“Who?”

The producer pointed at the Pomeranian.

The assistant stared.

“The dog?”

“The dog.”

“That’s stupid.”

“It tested extremely well.”

—

By Day Three, paranoia had metastasized.

The accountant had concluded that the British man was straight because he “seemed too comfortable around throw pillows.”

The British man suspected Silk Shirt because he said “buddy” too often.

Silk Shirt suspected the bodybuilder because he owned a pickup truck.

The bodybuilder suspected everyone.

Present Cage suspected production.

1997 Cage suspected Present Cage.

Mustache Cage suspected causality itself.

And a contestant named Trevor, who had somehow avoided attention completely, was just trying to survive.

Trevor worked in commercial insurance.

He had been told by casting:

“Just act natural.”

Then, after arriving:

“Actually, act gay.”

Then:

“Actually, act natural while acting gay.”

He had not emotionally recovered.

At dinner, Trevor ordered steak.

The entire table stopped.

Trevor froze.

“What?”

The accountant slowly said:

“Steak.”

“Yes.”

“Interesting.”

Trevor slammed both hands on the table.

“STOP SAYING INTERESTING.”

Everyone recoiled.

Trevor pointed around the room.

“You know what? Fine. I like steak. I also like musicals. I hate football. I own three power drills. I cried at Coco. None of this means anything!”

Silence.

Present Cage slowly stood.

“My God.”

Trevor looked terrified.

“What?”

Cage pointed at him.

“He understands.”

“Understands what?”

“The categories are collapsing.”

“No! I’m just tired!”

Cage turned to the others.

“That’s what they want!”

Trevor shouted:

“WHO IS THEY?”

All three Nicolas Cages answered simultaneously:

“THE PRODUCERS.”

Mustache Cage looked pleased.

“Good harmony.”

—

That evening came the elimination ceremony.

The contestants stood beneath dramatic lighting.

The host held a gold envelope.

“Tonight, you must identify the straight man.”

Each contestant wrote down a name.

Present Cage wrote:

THE HOST

1997 Cage wrote:

NICOLAS CAGE

Then crossed it out.

Then wrote:

OTHER NICOLAS CAGE

Mustache Cage wrote:

THE FORMAT

The bodybuilder wrote:

BRITISH GUY

The British man wrote:

BODYBUILDER

Silk Shirt wrote:

TREVOR

The accountant wrote:

TREVOR

Trevor wrote:

ME???

The host opened the votes.

He stared at them.

Then into the camera.

Then back at the votes.

“These are… unusually unhelpful.”

Present Cage stepped forward.

“You lied to us.”

The host smiled nervously.

“About what?”

“There is no straight man.”

A gasp went through the contestants.

The host’s smile disappeared.

Present Cage continued.

“We’re all straight.”

Another gasp.

The bodybuilder looked genuinely betrayed.

“You’re straight?”

“Yes!”

“You told me you loved Cher.”

“I DO!”

“Then what does anything mean?”

“NOTHING!”

The accountant suddenly sat down.

“I need water.”

The host raised his hands.

“Okay. Fine.”

The music stopped.

“So you figured it out.”

Cage smiled.

“Obviously.”

The host turned toward the camera.

“Then I suppose it’s time to reveal the real twist.”

Cage’s smile vanished.

“No.”

1997 Cage whispered:

“No.”

Mustache Cage whispered:

“Yes.”

The host pointed toward the Pomeranian.

“The only contestant who knew the full premise…”

Everyone slowly turned.

The dog yawned.

“…was Biscuit.”

The bodybuilder stared at his dog.

“You knew?”

Biscuit scratched his ear.

The host explained.

“Biscuit was trained to press one of two buttons depending on whether contestants were relaxed or performing.”

Two buttons were wheeled out.

One said:

AUTHENTIC

The other said:

TRYING TOO HARD

Trevor looked like he was going to cry.

“You gave the dog editorial control?”

“Partial editorial control.”

Biscuit walked directly to the TRYING TOO HARD button and pressed it.

A buzzer sounded.

Everyone looked at Present Cage.

Cage looked offended.

“What?”

The host said:

“That was for you.”

Cage pointed at the dog.

“He has an agenda.”

Biscuit pressed TRYING TOO HARD again.

1997 Cage started laughing.

Present Cage turned.

“You think this is funny?”

Biscuit pressed TRYING TOO HARD a third time.

Mustache Cage nearly spit out his drink.

—

The finale took place the next morning.

All eight contestants stood together.

The host addressed the cameras.

“So what did we learn?”

The accountant answered immediately.

“Never trust casting.”

The bodybuilder said:

“Clothes mean less than I thought.”

The British man said:

“Americans desperately need better reality television.”

Trevor said:

“I’m going home.”

Silk Shirt shrugged.

“I had fun.”

1997 Cage looked at Present Cage.

“You?”

Present Cage thought for a moment.

“That humans become extremely strange when they believe ordinary behavior is evidence.”

The host nodded.

“That’s… unexpectedly insightful.”

Mustache Cage added:

“And dogs are tyrants.”

Biscuit pressed TRYING TOO HARD.

Mustache Cage pointed.

“You see?”

The host smiled at the camera.

“And that concludes Who’s the Straight Guy?”

Present Cage raised a hand.

“One final question.”

“Yes?”

“Why were there three Nicolas Cages?”

The host paused.

“We only cast one.”

Silence.

Present Cage looked at 1997 Cage.

1997 Cage looked at Mustache Cage.

Mustache Cage finished his martini.

“Well.”

Present Cage sighed.

“No.”

1997 Cage smiled.

“Oh yes.”

The accountant stood up.

“I’m leaving before this becomes another show.”

Biscuit pressed:

AUTHENTIC.

Everyone looked at the dog.

The accountant pointed at him.

“Thank you.”

And for the first time all week, nobody overthought it.

The funnier engine here is performance contaminating evidence: the harder everyone tries to “look like” something, the less diagnostic anything becomes. Then Biscuit becomes the only decent observer because he isn’t reasoning about stereotypes at all—he’s just watching who is tense.